\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RExistRefvarInsert
< m1: NPPROP{NPPROPrec1}
            [ mu1,
              objrel/T2,
              I2: mu2,
              I3: mu3
            ] 

  m2: T1 
>

< m : NPPROP{NPPROPrec1}
            [ mu1,
              objrel/T2,
              I2: mu2,
              I4: tempadvrel/T1,
              I3: mu3
            ] 
>
   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I2: ALL (mu2, locadvrel/..)
       I3: QUOTE_temprefnotfound(mu3) AND
           QUOTE_tempretronotfound(mu3) AND
           QUOTE_tempdurnotfound(mu3) AND
           NOT EXIST (mu3, [I31:rel1/..]) 
         I31: rel1 IN [tempadvrel, locadvrel]
       I4: T1.CAT IN [ADVPVAR, PREPPVAR, SENTENCEVAR]
       m2: T1.CAT IN [ADVPVAR, PREPPVAR, SENTENCEVAR]
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. ADVPVAR *)
      < T1: ADVPVAR{ADVPVARrec1} >
      < T1: ADVPVAR{ADVPVARrec1} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: ADVPVARrec1.class = reference AND
              ADVPVARrec1.retro = false
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: ADVPVARrec1.class = reference AND
              ADVPVARrec1.retro = false
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. PREPPVAR *)
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: PREPPVARrec1.class = reference AND
              PREPPVARrec1.retro = false
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: PREPPVARrec1.class = reference AND
              PREPPVARrec1.retro = false
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 3. SENTENCEVAR *)
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1.conjclass = reference AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1.conjretro = false
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1.conjclass = reference AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1.conjretro = false
          A1: @
         >
>

&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RExistRefVarInsert
\item[* relevant examples:]  
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] ExistPropFormation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_
Exist(Temp)AdvVar
\item[* date of creation:] 12-SEP-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch RExistRefvarInsertion
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 23/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted rule so that it will work in a large iterative class containing all 
advvar insertion rules. Especially:\\
1) there is only one var-extraction order in analysis (tempadv - locadv - 
sentadv), correlated with the actual surface order of the vars (which is locadv 
- tempadv (sentadv is only in leftdislocrel for the moment), so there is right 
to left extraction)\\
2) in generation, there are more orders possible for insertion (depending on 
source language)\\
3) the surface order for elements of the same 
class remains the same over languages, with obligatory left to right insertion 
(locadv1 - locadv2 - locadv3 etc) = right to left extraction
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] isomorphism with other subgrammars
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
Note that for existentials, only one kind tempadv is allowed (a refvar). In 
case this is adapted, mu2 must be made to allow for tempadvs as well, with a 
further restriction on the kind of tempadv allowed there.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:] 
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}

% RULE RExistConjsentVar
< m1: NPPROP{NPPROPrec1}[I1:mu1] 

  m2: T1
>

< m : NPPROP{NPPROPrec1}
            [ leftdislocrel/T1,
              I2:mu1
            ] 
>


   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: NOT EXIST (mu1, [leftdislocrel/..])
       I2: NOT EXIST (mu1, [I21:rel1/..])
         I21: rel1 IN [leftdislocrel, tempadvrel, locadvrel,
                       postsentadvrel]
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. SENTENCE *)
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = true AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1.temporal  = false
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = true AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1.temporal  = false
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. PREPP *)
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       T1: PREPPVARrec1.synppefs * [synNP, synOPENGERUND] <> []
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RExistConjsentVar
\item[* relevant examples:] Although they don't exist anymore, there were
unicorns (once); Without wanting to upset you, there are hackers
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] ExistPropFormation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class)
 RC\_Exist(Sent)AdvVar
\item[* date of creation:] 7-MAR-1989 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] RIdentConjsentVar
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 23/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted rule to make it work in one large iterative advvar rule class: sentadvs 
and conjsents are the last advvars to be removed from the sentence in analysis 
(in 
generation, the insertion order is determined by the source language). See the 
remarks made under RExistRefVarInsert.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to insert a variable for an adverbial sentence in initial 
position
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) The condition 
on no other leftdislocrels makes that this rule cannot co-occur with 
RExistSentAdvVar. If something like that is still needed, the {\em position\/} 
attribute of the sentadv will have to be used to put it somewhere else.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
Note that the ordering 
of conjsents may be relevant for pronominal reference; therefore, the strategy 
in IL is to preserve the ordering of (conj)sents as much as possible,
i.e. an initial conjsent is mapped to an initial conjsent in other languages 
as well (but only in the substitution rules, not in the var-insertion rules!).
 Dutch also has a `middle' conjsent in some subgrammars; since this 
seems unfelicitous in English, it is mapped onto an initial conjsent, and on a 
final one (in case there already is something else in leftdislocrel position), 
but with a lowered bonus.

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

