   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I2: NOT EXIST (mu1, [I21:rel1/..])
         I21: rel1 IN [tempadvrel, locadvrel]
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. SENTENCE *)
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = true AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1.temporal  = false
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = true AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1.temporal  = false
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. PREPP *)
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       T1: PREPPVARrec1.synppefs * [synNP, synOPENGERUND] <> []
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RExistFinalConjsentVar
\item[* relevant examples:] (there were) unicorns (once), although they don't 
exist anymore; ? (There is no) victim, without considering all the details of 
the crime
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] ExistPropFormation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) 
RC\_Exist(Sent)AdvVar
\item[* date of creation:] 9-MAR-1989 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] RIdentConjsentVar
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 23/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted rule to make it work in one large iterative advvar rule class: sentadvs 
are the last advvars to be removed from the sentence in analysis (in 
generation, the insertion order is determined by the source language). See the 
remarks made under RExistRefVarInsert.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:]
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) There may be 
more than one postsentadvrel 
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
Dutch assumed an attribute {\em distri\/}(bution) for CONJs, indicating 
whether a CONJ can occur sentence initially and/or finally. In the substitution
rules (where the CONJ is visible), there is a test on correct occurrence. For
English, no examples could be found of CONJs restricted to final use (they all 
seem to be allowed initially), but still this condition has been built in (see 
the proposition substitution rules).
\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
Note that the ordering 
of conjsents may be relevant for pronominal reference; therefore, the strategy 
in IL is to preserve the ordering of (conj)sents as much as possible,
i.e. the propsubst rule for an initial conjsent is mapped to the propsubst rule 
for an initial conjsent in other languages 
as well. Dutch also has a `middle' conjsent in some subgrammars; since this 
seems unfelicitous in English, it is mapped onto an initial conjsent; in case 
there already is something there, it is also mapped onto a final conjsent, but 
with a lowered bonus.
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RExistSentadvVar
< m1: NPPROP{NPPROPrec1}[ I1:mu1 ]

  m2: T2
>

< m :NPPROP{NPPROPrec1}
                [ leftdislocrel/T2,
                  I2:mu1
                ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: NOT EXIST (mu1, [leftdislocrel/..])
       I2: NOT EXIST (mu1, [I11:rel1/..])
         I11: rel1 IN [locadvrel, tempadvrel, leftdislocrel,
                       postsentadvrel]
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. ADVP *)
      < T2: ADVPVAR{ADVPVARrec1} >
      < T2: ADVPVAR{ADVPVARrec1} >

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       T2: ADVPVARrec1.actsubcefs * [sentadv, causadv] <> []
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. PREPPVAR *)
      < T2: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >
      < T2: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       T2: caus IN PREPPVARrec1.actsubcefs
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
